<header><p class="st-kicker">chiefdom · Maritime Southeast Asia</p><h1 class="st-title">Namayan</h1><p class="st-meta">1175 CE – 1571 CE (approx.) · capital Sapa</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A confederation of Tagalog settlements on the Pasig River, centred at Sapa in today's Santa Ana district of Manila.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Namayan is known from Spanish Franciscan accounts and from archaeology at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/santa-ana-manila">Santa Ana</a> church, where burials with Chinese ceramics of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song</a> and Ming periods were found. Tradition, recorded centuries later, dates its rise to 1175 and names its early rulers as Lakan Tagkan and his wife Kalangitan. The date and names cannot be confirmed.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Namayan was a loose league of barangays along the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pasig-river">Pasig River</a> and the shores of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/laguna-de-bay">Laguna de Bay</a>, linked by kinship and trade. Its people farmed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rice">rice</a>, fished and traded with Chinese merchants, and like <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-tondo">Tondo</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-maynila">Maynila</a> it was led by a hereditary nobility.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>After the Spanish took <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-maynila">Maynila</a> in 1571 Namayan came under their rule, and in 1578 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/franciscans">Franciscans</a> founded the mission of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/santa-ana-manila">Santa Ana</a> de Sapa there. Its lands became the districts of Santa Ana, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pandacan">Pandacan</a> and others of modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manila">Manila</a>.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/namayan.jpg" alt="Namayan" width="320" height="349"><figcaption>Image: Delirium333 · CC BY 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Map_of_Namayan.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1175 CE – 1571 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>chiefdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Sapa</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Indigenous Philippine religion</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Old Tagalog</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>Philippines</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Sapa, Kingdom of Namayan</dd></dl></aside></div>

